## Purpose

Enforce recipient consent/capability checks on every relay first-contact delivery path before durable routing.

## Work

- Require valid recipient-issued authority in production before any durable first-contact enqueue. Prefer one backend admission operation (B261 `admit_contact_delivery` shape) rather than handler-only checks before `R.send`.
- Close all three live bypasses: `handle_send`/`R.send`, `handle_send_all`/`R.send_all`, and `handle_forward` → local `R.send`. Source-relay authentication is not recipient consent.
- Preserve Ed25519 sender authentication and alias binding; do not treat sender authentication as recipient consent.
- Apply identical validation to HTTP, WebSocket, connector outbox/sync, direct relay CLI, retries, and any batch/room-adjacent DM path. Connector inbound policy remains defence in depth only (post-accept).
- Secondary effects only on first accepted admission: `stats_note_message`, `Relay_ws_server.push_dm`, short-queue, observers. Note: `handle_send` currently treats `` `Ok `` and `` `Duplicate `` alike for pushes — fix for contact delivery.
- Rejected grants must never content-DLQ on either backend (InMemory local-send currently does for unknown/dead; Sqlite does not).
- Bind grants to intended recipient/host and sender; reject guessed, leaked-but-bound-elsewhere, expired, revoked, replayed, malformed, wrong-version, and downgraded authority.
- Enumeration-resistant errors; rate/size limits after admission only.
- Design input: `.collab/research/2026-07-22-b261-private-reachability-persistence-reconciliation.md`.

## Acceptance evidence

- An authenticated but unauthorised relay peer cannot deliver by knowing or guessing a private alias.
- Deliberate route/capability sharing permits only the intended scope.
- Revocation/expiry/replay take effect under concurrent requests and across restarts.
- Route and backend parity tests prove no bypass path remains across `/send`, `/send_all`, and `/forward`.